(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-14-knowledge-management-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 14: Knowledge Management Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"You are finishing residency and will begin practice. You want to establish a plan for keeping up with new developments in your field even though there are few professional colleagues in your community. All of the following might be useful, but which will be most useful to you?","choices":["Subscribe to a few good journals.","Subscribe to a service that reviews the literature in your field.","Buy new editions of printed textbooks.","Search MEDLINE at regular intervals.","Keep up contacts with colleagues in your training program by e-mail and telephone."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is virtually impossible to keep up with all scientifically strong, clinically relevant new research alone, so a service that reviews the literature in your field does the screening and summarizing for you, which matters most when few colleagues are nearby. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.1"},{"q":"You can rely on the best general medical journals in your field to:","choices":["Provide answers to clinical questions.","Assure that you have kept up with the medical literature.","Guarantee that the information they contain is beyond reproach.","Expose you to the many dimensions of your profession."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The best general journals are a rich source of information on the many dimensions of being a physician, including history, politics, science, ideas, and experiences, even though they are not good for immediate answers or surveillance. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.2"},{"q":"Many children in your practice have attacks of otitis media. You want to base your management on the best available evidence. Which of the following is the least credible source of information on this question?","choices":["A clinical practice guideline by a major medical society","A systematic review published in a major journal","The Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews","The most recent research article on this question"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A single recent article, seen out of context with the other studies on the same question, sits lower in the hierarchy than syntheses and is of marginal value unless it is much stronger than all the studies before it. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.3"},{"q":"A search of MEDLINE is especially useful for which of the following?","choices":["Finding all of the best articles bearing on a clinical question","An efficient strategy for finding the good articles","Looking for reports of rare events","Keeping up with the medical literature","Being familiar with the medical profession as a whole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A targeted search is indispensable for learning whether a rare event has been reported, because a specific query can locate even a single case report among millions of citations; it is too inefficient for most questions at the point of care. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is accomplished by peer review of research manuscripts before they are published?","choices":["Exclude articles by authors with a conflict of interest.","Make the published article accurate and trustworthy.","Relieve readers of the need to be skeptical about the study.","Decide for the editors about whether they should publish the manuscript."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as the best available answer. Peer review and editing make articles more readable, accurate, and complete, and it is an accepted marker of quality, but articles remain imperfect after publication. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.5"},{"q":"An author of an article showing that screening colonoscopy is more effective in preventing colorectal cancer than other forms of screening would have conflicts of interest if he or she had any of the following except:","choices":["Clinical income from performing colonoscopies","Investment in a company that makes colonoscopies","Investment in medical products in general","Publications of articles that have consistently advocated colonoscopy as the best screening test","Rivalry with other scholars who advocate another screening test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Conflict of interest relates to a specific activity, and investing in medical products in general is not tied to colonoscopy in particular, so it creates no specific conflict for this conclusion. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is the least useful way of looking up answers to clinical questions at the point of care?","choices":["Subscribing to several journals and keeping them available where you see patients","Guidelines on http://www.guidelines.gov","The Cochrane Library on the Internet","A continually updated electronic textbook"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Journals have great value for other reasons, but a stack of them at the bedside is not a fast way to look up an answer, because they mostly report single studies rather than an organized, evidence-graded answer. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.7"},{"q":"Which of the following should be least reassuring to a patient about the quality of a web site providing information about HIV?","choices":["The site is sponsored by a governmental agency and names its advisory board members.","The site provides facts, not opinions.","The primary source of information is stated.","The date of the last revision is posted and recent.","The author is a well-known expert in the field."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Fame alone is the least reassuring, because a well-known expert may still be biased; the retracted Lancet vaccine study came from an author with undisclosed conflicts. Look for the sponsoring organization, facts, and stated sources. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.8"},{"q":"Which of the following is not part of grading clinical recommendations using the GRADE system?","choices":["Deciding whether to use a diagnostic test","Takes into account the balance of benefits and harms","Rates the quality of scientific evidence separately","Suggests how commonly and how forcefully a treatment should be recommended","Rates the strength of the evidence and of recommendations separately"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The grading table rates recommendations for treatment by the quality of the evidence and the strength of the recommendation; it is a way to grade evidence, and deciding whether to order a particular diagnostic test remains a clinical judgment rather than a component of the grading. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.9"},{"q":"A comprehensive approach to managing knowledge in your field would include which of the following?","choices":["Subscribing to some journals and browsing them","Establishing a plan for looking up information at the point of care","Finding a publication that helps you keep up with new developments in your field","Identifying web sites you can recommend to your patients","All of the above"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Browsing journals, a plan for point-of-care lookup, a publication that keeps you current, and reliable web sites for patients are all basic elements of a comprehensive knowledge management plan. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.10"}]};
